.TH OPENP2S 1 "2026" "openp2s" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
openp2s \- Azure Point-to-Site VPN client with Microsoft Entra ID authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openp2s
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openp2s
connects to an Azure Point-to-Site VPN gateway that uses Microsoft Entra ID
authentication, from Linux, using upstream OpenVPN with a minimal
credential-capacity patch.
.PP
Stock OpenVPN cannot carry an Entra access token through the authentication
exchange, because two independent limits are too small for it: the retained
credential buffer
.RB ( USER_PASS_LEN ,
128 bytes) truncates the password the token is sent as, and the key-method-2
control-channel buffer
.RB ( TLS_CHANNEL_BUF_SIZE ,
2048 bytes) cannot carry the message containing it. Raising either alone is
not sufficient. The OpenVPN shipped alongside this program enlarges both and
changes nothing else; it is installed at
.IR /usr/lib/openp2s/openvpn .
.PP
That binary also carries an opt-in Azure compatibility option, compiled in but
inert. OpenP2S does not use it: measurement against a live gateway showed no
Azure-specific protocol behaviour is required. It is there so that a gateway
which does require the Azure options string can be reached with
.B \-\-experimental\-azure\-compat
rather than a rebuilt OpenVPN. Only the local configuration can enable it \(em
a server cannot push it, and no environment variable affects it.
.PP
No TLS ClientHello shaping or key-logging code is compiled in at all.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI connect " profile.xml"
Authenticate, bring up the tunnel and apply the profile's split DNS. Runs in
the foreground; Ctrl+C disconnects cleanly.
.TP
.BI convert " profile.xml"
Write a standalone OpenVPN configuration. The output embeds the profile
serversecret as an inline tls-auth key and is written with mode 0600.
.TP
.BI inspect " profile.xml"
Show what OpenP2S reads from a profile. Never prints the serversecret.
.TP
.BI probe " profile.xml"
Run the authentication and protocol exchange against the gateway and report
how far it got, without creating a tunnel or altering routes or DNS. The
generated configuration uses
.BR "dev null" ,
.B route\-noexec
and
.BR ifconfig\-noexec ,
so the run cannot change host network state whatever privileges it has. It
does write a configuration and a socket into a private, throwaway directory,
and may update the cached Entra session.
.TP
.BI doctor " [profile.xml]"
Check the environment, the profile and any live connection.
.TP
.B status
Show the current connection.
.TP
.B disconnect
Disconnect and clean up.
.TP
.BI "auth login" " profile.xml"
Sign in and cache the Entra session without connecting.
.TP
.B auth status
Show cached Entra sessions.
.TP
.BR "auth clear " [ \-\-all ]
Forget a cached Entra session.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/user/$UID/openp2s/
Runtime state on tmpfs. The directory is mode 0700 and holds the generated
OpenVPN configuration and the management socket. When the compatibility
fallback described under
.B SECURITY
is used, the temporary credentials file lives here too, mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/openp2s/cache/
Persistent Entra token cache. The directory is mode 0700; the files in it are
mode 0600.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/openp2s/openvpn
The patched OpenVPN binary.
.SH PRIVILEGES
.B openp2s
runs as you, and asks for elevation only where root is genuinely required: the
.BR openvpn (8)
process lifecycle, and per-link DNS configuration. Those are invoked through
.BR sudo (8)
by absolute path.
.PP
Running it as
.B sudo openp2s
is refused. Serving your runtime directory and token cache while running as
root means creating and inspecting paths you control, and a path you control
can be replaced with a link to one you do not own \(em which would turn a
routine ownership fix into a way to acquire root-owned files. Nothing is lost
by refusing: the operations that need root still get it.
.SH SECURITY
OpenP2S normally delivers the Entra access token to OpenVPN over a private
Unix-domain socket, in memory. On that path the token is not written to any
file.
.PP
If the OpenVPN in use does not support that mechanism, OpenP2S falls back to a
temporary credentials file, created mode 0600 in the tmpfs runtime directory
above. It exists for the lifetime of the connection, because OpenVPN may
re-read it, and is removed during teardown, including when a connection
fails.
.PP
On either path the token never appears in argv or in the environment, and
never in the generated OpenVPN configuration.
.PP
Certificate verification is never disabled: there is no option to turn it off,
the gateway certificate is always validated against the system trust store,
and no CA is ever read from the profile.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A general error, or a query whose answer is negative.
.B openp2s status
returns 1 when no tunnel is connected, and
.B openp2s doctor
returns 1 when it finds a problem.
.TP
.B 2
The profile could not be parsed or failed validation.
.TP
.B 3
Entra authentication failed.
.TP
.B 4
The tunnel failed.
.TP
.B 5
Host network state could not be configured, or could not be fully cleaned up.
.B openp2s disconnect
returns 5 when teardown left something behind, and keeps the session record so
the attempt can be repeated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openvpn (8),
.BR resolvectl (1)
